# The tui-tools terminal UIs for the things a server administrator does.
#
# One addon for the whole family, from the signed pacman repository the tools
# publish themselves (https://pkgs.tui.tools/arch/$arch). It replaces the
# per-tool `tui-firewall` and `tui-systemd` addons, which built two of these
# tools from source inside the ISO builder and served them out of
# [omarchy-server]: the tools now have a release channel of their own, signed
# by their own key, and rebuilding somebody else's software to hand it to a
# user is a maintenance debt with no upside.
#
# Every one of them is a single static Go binary that shells out to the tool
# the machine already has (ufw, systemctl, journalctl, pacman, snapper, ssh,
# smbd, podman/docker). No daemon, no listening socket, no setuid path: what
# they add to the attack surface is the code you run when you sit at the
# console, which is why this is an addon and not part of the base.
#
# install/server/addons/tui-tools.preflight.sh is what configures the
# repository and pins its signing key BEFORE any of this is installed.
#
# The list is explicit rather than the `tui-tools-all` metapackage: naming the
# members is what tells the ISO builder which packages to bundle in its
# offline mirror. `tui-tools` itself is the launcher, a dashboard of the
# family that installs, updates and opens the others through pacman; it
# depends on none of them, so it is listed like any other member.
tui-tools
tui-cert
tui-containers
tui-cron
tui-disk
tui-firewall
tui-logs
tui-network
tui-samba
tui-secure
tui-snapper
tui-ssh
tui-systemd
tui-update
tui-users
tui-vpn
tui-traffic
tui-dc
